From: OpenWrt MT6990 repair <mt6990@local>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mediatek: reserve MT6990 RX DMA headroom

MT6990 ADMA can return the complete configured 2112-byte receive length.
The DMA address starts after the 64-byte skb headroom, but the generic
fragment calculation leaves only 2048 bytes between that address and
skb_shared_info. A 2112-byte receive therefore overwrites the shared-info
area and skb_put() catches it as skb_over_panic.

Keep the descriptor/DMA payload size unchanged and extend only the backing
fragment by the aligned skb headroom. This makes the entire advertised DMA
range valid without changing the hardware contract.

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
@@ -3207,6 +3207,11 @@ static int mtk_rx_alloc(struct mtk_eth *
 
 	ring->frag_size = mtk_max_frag_size(rx_data_len);
 	ring->buf_size = mtk_max_buf_size(ring->frag_size);
+	/* ADMA starts at data + headroom but may consume the complete buffer
+	 * length. Keep skb_shared_info beyond that DMA-visible range.
+	 */
+	if (MTK_HAS_CAPS(eth->soc->caps, MTK_SOC_MT6990))
+		ring->frag_size += SKB_DATA_ALIGN(NET_SKB_PAD + NET_IP_ALIGN);
 	ring->data = kcalloc(rx_dma_size, sizeof(*ring->data),
 			     GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!ring->data)
